#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a8f7a45-4d82-58b9-bd8a-4a39eb05db24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-3,\\,b=⟨6,6,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331878} {:answer/id #uuid "c7782099-a0fd-53c6-86da-c019401e1704" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=12,\\,b=⟨6,4,-6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331879} {:answer/id #uuid "49ef3c52-2524-518f-b2f3-1a0a54daaf75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨6,4,-6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331880} {:answer/id #uuid "958eab3e-848e-5daa-8090-2f55167ef79a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=6,\\,b=⟨4,6,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331881} {:answer/id #uuid "b33ba095-c0ae-5126-8eb9-0d3719014ba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨6,6,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b33ba095-c0ae-5126-8eb9-0d3719014ba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨6,6,4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331882} :answer-field/id #uuid "639a9cca-ac75-56eb-870d-145fc313d159" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "583166f3-f984-5b08-b009-d0467651a92a" :question/math-academy-id "q-129215" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $2x-3z=0$ and $2y-3z-6=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x-0=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n2y-0-6=0\\,⟹\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨0,3,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,0,-3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2y-3z-6=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,-3⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+6j+4k \\\\  & =⟨6,6,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,3,0⟩+t⟨6,6,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0+3=3,$ and $b=⟨6,6,4⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3b9f991-4144-574d-b26f-5126b1b33b4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331885} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ec36b8-e877-5158-9f63-24320f697d1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331886} {:answer/id #uuid "a5918a05-fcd8-5042-af53-53d9846fbcdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331887} {:answer/id #uuid "d66d9ffa-275f-5859-b1ba-449c843e4255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331888} {:answer/id #uuid "5d743de0-62e6-5fc0-80b4-ce5ef89010b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d66d9ffa-275f-5859-b1ba-449c843e4255" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331888} :answer-field/id #uuid "b141f848-4c27-5b0c-bf2a-351e8d6af56b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "959d7c1a-33e8-592b-b5a5-d7f6a288fc45" :question/math-academy-id "q-129241" :question/problem "Given the two planes $x-3y+2z=0$ and $x-2y+5=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes intersect on a line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-3y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-3,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-2,1⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨4,2,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}]}
